Here is the full audit of `UserRepositoryTests.cs`.

---

## Summary

**8 findings**: 3 Critical · 2 High · 1 Medium · 2 Low

5 of 8 tests are unreliable — they would pass even if the production code were broken. This is consistent with the incident you described.

---

## Critical — Tests giving false confidence

### 1. No Assertion — `GetUser_NonExistentId_ReturnsNull` (line 20–25)

The test calls `GetById(999)` but never asserts the result is `null`. It always passes.

```csharp
// BEFORE (always passes)
var user = repo.GetById(999);
// should be null

// AFTER
var user = repo.GetById(999);
Assert.IsNull(user);
```

### 2. Self-Referential Assertion — `DeleteUser_RemovesFromRepository` (line 49)

`Assert.AreEqual(repo.Count, repo.Count)` compares the property to **itself**. This is tautologically true regardless of whether `Delete` did anything.

```csharp
// BEFORE (always passes)
Assert.AreEqual(repo.Count, repo.Count);

// AFTER — verify the user is actually gone
Assert.AreEqual(0, repo.Count);
Assert.IsNull(repo.GetById(1));
```

### 3. Swallowed Exception — `AddUser_DuplicateId_ThrowsException` (lines 28–41)

The `try/catch` pattern has two problems:
- If the duplicate `Add` does **not** throw, the test silently passes (falls through).
- The `catch (Exception)` is overbroad — any exception satisfies it.

```csharp
// BEFORE (passes even when no exception is thrown)
try { repo.Add(new User(1, "Bob")); }
catch (Exception) { return; }

// AFTER
Assert.ThrowsException<InvalidOperationException>(
    () => repo.Add(new User(1, "Bob")));
```

---

## High — Will cause pain soon

### 4. Broad Exception Type — `AddUser_NullUser_ThrowsArgumentNullException` (line 80)

The method name promises `ArgumentNullException`, but the assertion catches any `Exception`. If `Add` threw `NullReferenceException` or `InvalidOperationException` this test would still pass.

```csharp
// BEFORE
Assert.ThrowsException<Exception>(() => repo.Add(null!));

// AFTER — match the actual thrown type
Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentNullException>(() => repo.Add(null!));
```

### 5. Negated Assertion — `UpdateUser_ChangesName` (line 59)

`Assert.IsTrue(user.Name != "Alice")` only proves the name is no longer `"Alice"`. If `Update` set it to `""` or `"GARBAGE"`, this still passes.

```csharp
// BEFORE
Assert.IsTrue(user.Name != "Alice");

// AFTER — assert the expected value
Assert.AreEqual("Alicia", user.Name);
```

---

## Medium — Maintainability

### 6. Shallow Assertion — `GetUser_ExistingId_ReturnsUser` (line 16)

`Assert.IsNotNull(user)` confirms something was returned but not that it's the **right** user. If `GetById` returned a different user, this passes.

```csharp
// BEFORE
Assert.IsNotNull(user);

// AFTER
Assert.IsNotNull(user);
Assert.AreEqual(1, user.Id);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", user.Name);
```

---

## Low — Hygiene

| # | Anti-Pattern | Location | Fix |
|---|---|---|---|
| 7 | **Print debugging** | Line 92 — `Console.WriteLine(...)` | Remove the `Console.WriteLine`; it adds noise to test output. |
| 8 | **Unused static resource** | Line 8 — `static readonly HttpClient _client` | Never used, never disposed. Delete the field. |

---

## Positive Observations

- **`GetAllUsers_ReturnsCorrectCount`** (line 62–74) is well-written: verifies count and each element by name with clear AAA structure.
- **`SearchUsers_ByPartialName_FindsMatches`** has a correct assertion (minor style note: prefer `Assert.AreEqual(2, results.Count)` for better failure messages).
- Test naming follows `Method_Scenario_Expected` convention consistently.
- Each test creates its own `UserRepository` — good isolation, no shared mutable state.

---

## Recommended Fix Priority

| Priority | Tests | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Fix now** | `GetUser_NonExistentId_ReturnsNull`, `DeleteUser_RemovesFromRepository`, `AddUser_DuplicateId_ThrowsException` | These 3 Critical tests pass regardless of correctness — exactly the class of bug behind your incident. |
| **Fix this sprint** | `AddUser_NullUser_ThrowsArgumentNullException`, `UpdateUser_ChangesName` | Overbroad/negated assertions will mask regressions. |
| **Fix opportunistically** | Shallow assertion in `GetUser_ExistingId_ReturnsUser`, remove `Console.WriteLine`, remove unused `HttpClient` | Low risk but easy wins. |
